    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 141735\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1035 AM MST Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n.UPDATE...18Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Abundant moisture will remain situated across the eastern half\n  of Arizona providing enhanced chances for showers and \n  thunderstorms through the middle portion of the week, especially\n  over the mountain locations.\n\n- There will be the potential for some storms to produce strong\n  winds, but the bigger threats will be heavy rainfall and \n  localized flash flooding over the next few days.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected\n  throughout the entire week. \n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nThe next few days should be fairly active with periods of showers\nand thunderstorms and an increased threat of heavy rainfall and\nflash flooding. Current objective analysis shows the subtropical\nridge now planted over the Lower MS River Valley while a deep\nPacific trough is moving through the Northern Rockies/Southwest\nCanada. This places our region within a somewhat drier\nsouthwesterly upper level flow, but southerly moist low and mid \nlevel flow. So, despite the drier air aloft that is coming in from\nthe west, the low and mid level moist advection is keeping PWATs \nanywhere from 1.4-1.8\" across south-central into eastern Arizona. \nSurface dew points well into the 60s and low level mixing ratios \nbetween 12-14 g/kg are anticipated to last for another 2-3 days as\nthe synoptic pattern will largely remain unchanged.\n\nCurrent satellite and radar imagery shows a decaying complex of \nshowers and storms across southwest New Mexico and southeast \nArizona. To the west across south-central Arizona, the moist \nsoutherly flow is beginning to show some CU development across the\nPhoenix area into the central Arizona high terrain. CAMs suggest \nsome scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are likely to \ndevelop over the next few hours somewhere across Maricopa into \nGila County. This convection should stay elevated with the only \nthreat being localized heavy rainfall before diminishing by mid \nmorning. \n\nThe rest of today should provide plenty of sunshine to further\nbuild instability with HREF MUCAPEs reaching 1000-1500 J/kg from\nPhoenix eastward by mid afternoon. However, just like yesterday\nconvection is not quite expected to fully utilize the instability\nas the upper levels will remain fairly neutral with the best jet\nsupport across New Mexico and southeast Arizona. CAMs do indicate\ngood convective development across southeast Arizona during the\nlatter half of the afternoon with more isolated potential into the\nsouth-central Arizona lower deserts. The expectation for late \nthis afternoon into the evening is for better chances over the \nhigh terrain east and southeast of Phoenix with PoPs of 30-50% and\na 15-25% chance into the lower deserts. Forecast soundings \nindicate nearly uncapped environment late in the afternoon and \nmarginal DCAPEs which could result in a few strong wind gusts. The\nbiggest threat for today is likely to be heavy rainfall and \npotentially a localized threat of flash flooding across eastern \nPinal into southern Gila County. \n\nAfter an expected break in the action later this evening into the\nearly overnight, CAMs show another likely round of showers and\nstorms developing at any point after 3 AM Tuesday morning with the\nbest area of focus over the high terrain just northeast of\nPhoenix. This area may need a Flood Watch for Tuesday morning as\nthe HREF is showing a bullseye of over 1\" average QPF across\nnortheast Maricopa County.  \n\nAs upper level support increases on Tuesday and likely peaks\nduring the first half of Wednesday, we are likely to have off and\non scattered showers and storms anywhere across south-central and\neastern Arizona. The latest CAMs indicate one round of storms \nTuesday afternoon initiating somewhere south and southeast of the \nPhoenix area before spreading north northeastward into the evening\nhours. The main forecast concern for Tuesday and especially into \nWednesday will be the periods of showers and storms supporting a \nmarginal to a slight risk of excessive rainfall potentially \nleading to localized flash flooding. Moisture will remain \nplentiful and at least modest instability will support scattered \nthunderstorm activity into Wednesday. A Flood Watch is likely to \nbe needed for portions of the area on Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nElsewhere early this week, dry conditions will prevail across\nsoutheast California and likely across most of southwest Arizona.\nA dry line has already set up along the Lower CO River Valley and\nit is expected to attempt to push farther into Yuma and La Paz \nCounties at times through Wednesday. Although the CAMs do not show\nany convection forming across far southwest Arizona, it would not\nbe impossible to get an isolated strong storm or two across La \nPaz and/or Yuma Counties. Temperatures will continue to be \nslightly above normal across the western deserts with highs \naround 105 degrees, while more clouds and rain-induced cooling \nshould keep highs closer to 100 degrees across the south-central \nArizona lower deserts.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/...\nGuidance is starting to agree on a quicker drying trend later this\nweek as the Pacific trough is favored to move farther into the\nGreat Basin and at least into northern and western portions of the\nDesert Southwest. This is likely to push drier air eastward\nthrough most if not all of Arizona as early as Thursday but more\nso Friday into Saturday. Thursday is still a bit of an uncertain\nday as rain chances may still linger across the south-central\nlower deserts and definitely over eastern Arizona. However, by\nFriday, enough dry air moving in should end chances over the \nlower deserts and really limit chances over the high terrain. It \nis doubtful we will completely scour out all of the moisture \nacross the higher terrain, so 10-20% PoPs remain each day across \neastern Gila County through the weekend. \n\nAs conditions dry out into the weekend, temperatures are likely \nto face a bit of a warming trend but as of now the region is \nfavored to stay under broad upper level troughing into the \nweekend. Lower desert highs are expected to warm to above normal \nby Friday or Saturday, but so far the NBM indicates a most likely \nrange of 103-107 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nThe main impact will be periods of VCSH/VCTS, and SHRA/TSRA later\nthis afternoon (00-04Z) and once again tomorrow morning (10-14Z).\nConfidence remains low as early morning models had a similar \noutlook this morning, with nothing developing as expected. However,\nthe environment looks more favorable over the next couple of days\nbut monitoring and updates will need to follow. SHRA/TSRA will be\ncapable of producing locally gusty winds with a low chance \n(10-20%) for gusts >30 kts. A period of southerly cross winds will\ngive way to westerly flow by this afternoon (21-22Z) then back \nE/SE tonight. Wind speeds will mostly stay under 10 kts, outside \nof shower and storm influences. SCT to occasionally BKN cloud \ndecks around 8-10K ft AGL will be common through the TAF period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \n\nNo aviation weather concerns will exist through the TAF period\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds will continue to behave similar \nto the past 24 hours varying between southeasterly and westerly at\nKIPL and southerly to southwesterly at KBLH with periods gusts\nbetween 20-25 kts this afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of scattered showers and thunderstorms are likely through\nWednesday across the eastern districts supporting a localized \nheavy rainfall threat. Moisture levels will remain elevated except\nfor southeast California. Expect minimum humidity levels to still\nfall into a 15-25% range for the western districts while the \neastern districts will only dip to 30-45% through Wednesday. \nWinds will generally follow diurnal patterns across the eastern \ndistricts, while much of the western districts will have elevated \nsoutherly winds with occasional gusts above 20 mph. By Thursday\ninto Friday, much drier air is forecast to spread eastward across\nthe region, eventually ending rain chances for all but the far\neastern Arizona high terrain and lowering humidities into the\nteens across the lower deserts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
